`startChangeDetection(options)` is intentionally not awaited, but it also has no `.catch()` handler. If `options.presets.apply<StoryIndexGenerator>('storyIndexGenerator')` rejects or `indexGenerator.getIndex()` throws, the resulting rejected promise is unhandled, which emits an `unhandledRejection` warning (or crashes the process in stricter Node.js configurations). Add a `.catch((err) => logger.warn(...))` to surface the failure gracefully:
```ts
startChangeDetection(options).catch((err) => {
  logger.warn(`Change detection startup failed: ${err.message}`);
});
```

`newFiles.includes(storyFile)` performs an O(n) linear scan on every iteration of the inner loop. Since this runs once per `changedFile` × once per affected story file, the worst-case complexity is O(changedFiles × affectedStories × newFiles). Convert `newFiles` to a `Set<string>` at its declaration site (line 214) and use `newFilesSet.has(storyFile)` for O(1) lookups:
```ts
const newFilesSet = new Set(
  Array.from(changes.new).map((filePath) => resolve(repoRoot, filePath))
);
```
